<header class="main-header">
  <div class="series-title">Likutay Halachos &middot; Choshen Mishpat</div>
  <h1>Hilchos Dayonim 5</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">Laws of Judges &mdash; 5</div>
</header>

<div class="verse-block">
  Based on the teaching <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;Tik&rsquo;u Emunah&rdquo;</span> in Likutay [Moharan] II, Siman 5, Os 11
  <span class="source-ref">Concerning the blemish of judgment through unfit rabbis and judges, and its connection to the blemish of the covenant</span>
</div>

<!-- ====== OS 1 ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 1</div>

<p>
Based on what is explained in the teaching <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;Tik&rsquo;u Emunah&rdquo;</span> <span class="key-term-def">[&ldquo;Sound forth faith&rdquo;]</span> in Siman 5 in Likutay Tinyana <span class="key-term-def">[the second volume of Likutay Moharan]</span>, in Os 11: that there are times when <span class="key-term">mikreh</span> <span class="key-term-def">[a nocturnal occurrence / &ldquo;chance&rdquo;]</span> comes through rabbis and judges who are not fit, who blemish the <span class="scripture">&ldquo;thrones of judgment&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 122:5)</span>. Through this, the <span class="key-term">ahavos</span> <span class="key-term-def">[loves]</span> fall from the <span class="key-term">Merkavah</span> <span class="key-term-def">[Heavenly Chariot]</span> etc. And this is drawn down through the blemish of the <span class="key-term">t&rsquo;fillin</span> &mdash; through the raising of the aspect of the drop of <span class="key-term">Eisav</span> and <span class="key-term">Yishmael</span> to the brain etc.
</p>

<div class="word-note">
<strong>Key framework:</strong> This halachah connects three ideas from Likutay Moharan II:5 &mdash; (1) unfit judges blemish the &ldquo;thrones of judgment,&rdquo; (2) this causes the holy loves to fall from the Chariot, and (3) all of this is rooted in a blemish of the t&rsquo;fillin, i.e. impure forces (the &ldquo;drop of Eisav and Yishmael&rdquo;) ascending to the brain/mind. The entire halachah elaborates on why proper judgment is so difficult, why three judges are needed, and why the blemish of judgment and the blemish of the covenant (<em>bris</em>) are interdependent.
</div>

<!-- ====== OS 2 ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 2</div>

<p>
And therefore it is impossible to judge alone, for to clarify the judgment is exceedingly difficult and weighty. And as Shlomoh said: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;For who can judge this weighty people of Yours?&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(I Kings 3:9)</span>. And even Moshe Rabbeinu said: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;I cannot carry you alone&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 1:9)</span>. For the <span class="key-term">mishpat</span> <span class="key-term-def">[judgment]</span> has fallen greatly through the sin of Adam HaRishon and through the sins of the previous generations who blemished the judgment greatly.
</p>

<p>
For when one person denies his fellow&rsquo;s money and wishes to encroach upon his fellow&rsquo;s money unlawfully &mdash; this is the aspect of the blemish of judgment, the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;he makes wealth and not through justice&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Jeremiah 17:11)</span>. And through this is drawn down the blemish of the <span class="key-term">bris</span> <span class="key-term-def">[covenant]</span>, which comes through the blemish of judgment, as above in the aforementioned teaching. For the blemish of robbing one&rsquo;s fellow&rsquo;s money &mdash; which is the aspect of the blemish of judgment, where one takes his fellow&rsquo;s money unlawfully &mdash; and the blemish of the <span class="key-term">bris</span> are interdependent, as is explained in many places in his words, of blessed memory. And in particular in the teaching <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;G&rsquo;zailah&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Siman 69)</span>, as is explained there regarding the words of our Sages, of blessed memory: <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;Whoever descends into his fellow&rsquo;s trade is as if he had relations with his wife&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Sanhedrin 81a)</span>; see there.
</p>

<p>
And this is what is written regarding the Generation of the Flood: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;For all flesh had corrupted its way&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Genesis 6:12)</span> &mdash; which is the blemish of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>. And immediately adjacent it is written: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;The end of all flesh etc., for the earth is filled with <em>chamas</em> [robbery]&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Genesis 6:13)</span>. And Rashi explained: their decree was not sealed except on account of robbery. For this depends upon that &mdash; for through the proliferation of robbery, which is the blemish of judgment &mdash; and especially since the judges blemished the judgment greatly at that time, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said regarding the judges of Sedom &mdash; therefore that desire [i.e., the blemish of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>] became overpowering, in the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;for all flesh had corrupted&rdquo;</span> etc.
</p>

<p>
And therefore the essential sealing of their decree was specifically on account of robbery, which is the blemish of judgment &mdash; even though at first the Torah spoke only of the blemish of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, as it is written in the preceding passage: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;And Hashem saw&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Genesis 6:5)</span>, and likewise in this passage: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;And G-d saw the earth, and behold it was corrupted&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Genesis 6:12)</span> &mdash; so it appears that their essential wickedness was this, that this was exceedingly evil in His eyes, <span class="key-term">Yisborach</span>. Yet even so, their decree was not sealed except on account of robbery, which is the blemish of judgment. For when the blemish of the <span class="key-term">bris</span> becomes overpowering through the blemish of judgment, then it is exceedingly, exceedingly difficult to rectify, as is explained there in the aforementioned teaching. And therefore specifically through the robbery &mdash; which is the blemish of judgment &mdash; their decree was sealed regarding the immorality, for this depends upon that, as above. For when immorality comes through the blemish of judgment, then it is exceedingly difficult to rectify; therefore specifically through this their decree was sealed, as above.
</p>

<p>
And this is known in the writings of the Arizal, that the Exile of Egypt was to rectify the sin of the Generation of the Flood. And therefore, when Moshe saw an Egyptian man striking [a Jew] etc., and afterward two Jewish men quarreling etc. <span class="source">(Exodus 2:11&ndash;13)</span>, he said: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Indeed the matter is known&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Exodus 2:14)</span> &mdash; [i.e., now it is known] why Yisroel is in exile, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said <span class="source">(Shemos Rabbah 1:30)</span>. That is, because he saw that they still blemish the judgment &mdash; for the quarrel and strife is the opposite of judgment, which is the aspect of peace, as it is written: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;truth and the judgment of peace shall you judge&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Zechariah 8:16)</span>. Therefore he said: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Indeed the matter is known&rdquo;</span> &mdash; why Yisroel are placed in exile for so long, for this is on account of the blemish of judgment, which is exceedingly difficult to clarify.
</p>

<!-- ====== OS 3 ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 3</div>

<p>
For it is explained there in the aforementioned teaching that the essential blemish of judgment, through which comes the blemish of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, is through raising the aspect of the drop of Eisav and Yishmael to the brain etc. And on account of this it is exceedingly difficult and weighty to elevate and clarify the judgment. And therefore it is forbidden to judge alone, and three judges are required.
</p>

<p>
For these judges need to clarify and elevate the judgment from its fall &mdash; for it fell through one of these litigants wishing to rob his fellow, which is the aspect of the blemish of judgment, as above &mdash; through which comes <span class="key-term">mikreh laylah</span> <span class="key-term-def">[a nocturnal occurrence]</span>, see there. And when they clarify the judgment and elevate it, and return the money to its rightful place &mdash; to the one to whom it truly belongs &mdash; through this they elevate and clarify the blemish of the <span class="key-term">nitzotzos</span> <span class="key-term-def">[sparks]</span> that fell through the blemish of the <span class="key-term">mikreh</span>; they elevate them to the brain. And this is the aspect of the rectification of the <span class="key-term">t&rsquo;fillin</span>, which is drawn down through elevating from the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;engraved in his flesh&rdquo;</span> &mdash; which comes through the sword of arrogance &mdash; to the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;engravings of a signet&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Exodus 28:11)</span>.
</p>

<p>
However, one must be careful not to elevate excessively, so as not to raise to the brain, <em>chas v&rsquo;sholom</em>, the aspect of the drop of Eisav and Yishmael &mdash; for the essential blemish of judgment comes through this &mdash; through which the aforementioned desire returns and overpowers even more, as above. Therefore one person cannot judge alone, for the clarification of judgment requires, as it were, two opposites: to elevate from <span class="scripture">&ldquo;engraved in his flesh&rdquo;</span> to the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;engravings of a signet&rdquo;</span> &mdash; the aspect of <span class="key-term">t&rsquo;fillin</span> &mdash; and [simultaneously] to push away the aspect of the drop of Eisav and Yishmael so as not to blemish the judgment through them, as above.
</p>

<p>
Therefore, of necessity, two [judges] must sit who have opposing views &mdash; one inclines to the right and one to the left &mdash; through which these two aspects are accomplished: the aspect of <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;the left pushes away&rdquo;</span> and <span class="aramaic">&ldquo;the right draws near&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Sotah 47a)</span>, as above. And then the third one adjudicates, and the judgment emerges properly. See the aforementioned teaching well and understand.
</p>

<div class="word-note">
<strong>Structure of three judges:</strong> One inclines to the right (<em>yamin m&rsquo;kareves</em> = drawing near, elevating the sparks); one inclines to the left (<em>s&rsquo;mol docheh</em> = pushing away, rejecting the impure influence of Eisav/Yishmael); and the third adjudicates between them, ensuring the judgment emerges in proper balance. This is why a single judge cannot accomplish this &mdash; the task requires inherently opposing forces working together.
</div>

<!-- ====== OS 4 ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 4</div>

<p>
And this is what our Sages, of blessed memory, said: that the judge should see it as if <span class="key-term">Gehinnom</span> is open beneath him and a sword [is placed] between his thighs <span class="source">(Sanhedrin 7a; Yevamos 109b)</span>. And they derived this from the verse: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Behold the bed of Shlomoh etc., all of them grasping the sword, trained in war etc., because of dread in the nights&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Song of Songs 3:7&ndash;8)</span>.
</p>

<p>
<span class="scripture">&ldquo;Gehinnom is open beneath him&rdquo;</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of the judgment that has fallen to the lowest depths, which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">Gehinnom</span>, in the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Your judgments are a great deep&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Psalms 36:7)</span>. This is drawn down through raising to the brain the drop of Eisav and Yishmael, as above &mdash; and the judge needs to rectify this, to elevate the judgment from there.
</p>

<p>
<span class="scripture">&ldquo;And a sword is placed between his thighs&rdquo;</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of what the judge must clarify: the sword of arrogance, and to elevate from the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;engraved in his flesh&rdquo;</span> to the aspect of the <span class="scripture">&ldquo;signet&rdquo;</span> of holiness. For all this the judge must rectify, since the blemish of the <span class="key-term">bris</span> and the blemish of robbery &mdash; which is the blemish of judgment &mdash; are interdependent, as above.
</p>

<p>
And all this our Sages, of blessed memory, derived from the verse: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Behold the bed of Shlomoh&rdquo;</span> etc. &mdash; the aspect of <span class="key-term">mittah sh&rsquo;laimah</span> <span class="key-term-def">[a complete bed, i.e. the complete rectification of the covenant]</span> &mdash; the aspect of Yaakov, who is the aspect of the <span class="scripture">&ldquo;threefold cord&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Ecclesiastes 4:12)</span>, the aspect of the generality of the three judges, who are the aspect of the <span class="key-term">t&rsquo;las avahan</span> <span class="key-term-def">[three Patriarchs]</span> included in Yaakov who is the adjudicator &mdash; in the aspect of <span class="scripture">&ldquo;justice and righteousness in Yaakov&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Psalms 99:4)</span>. For he was completely clean of the blemish of the drop of Eisav and Yishmael. And therefore the essential rectification of judgment, which is the completeness of the rectification of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, is through him.
</p>

<p>
And this verse &mdash; <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Behold the bed of Shlomoh etc., because of dread in the nights&rdquo;</span> &mdash; is juxtaposed to the verse: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;His palanquin etc., its chariot of purple, its interior paved with love&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Song of Songs 3:9&ndash;10)</span> &mdash; which is the aspect of the bond of the <span class="key-term">Merkavah</span> [Chariot], which is the essential rectification of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, the rectification of judgment. Therefore they are juxtaposed to one another; see the aforementioned teaching and you will understand.
</p>

<!-- ====== OS 5 ====== -->
<div class="os-header">Os 5</div>

<p>
And the essential prolonging of the exile is through the blemish of judgment, through which the blemish of the <span class="key-term">bris</span> is drawn down even more &mdash; and this is exceedingly difficult to rectify, as is explained in the aforementioned teaching. And as it is written: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Those who turn justice to wormwood&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Amos 5:7)</span>. And it is written: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Her heads judge for bribes&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Michah 3:11)</span>. And likewise in many verses.
</p>

<p>
And this is what our Sages, of blessed memory, said <span class="source">(Eichah Rabbah, P&rsquo;sichta 4)</span>: Moshe said <span class="scripture">&ldquo;<em>Aichah</em> [how] shall I bear alone&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 1:12)</span>; Yeshayah said <span class="scripture">&ldquo;<em>Aichah</em> has she become a harlot&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Isaiah 1:21)</span>; and Yirmiyah said <span class="scripture">&ldquo;<em>Aichah</em> she sits&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Lamentations 1:1)</span>.
</p>

<p>
For Moshe said <span class="scripture">&ldquo;<em>Aichah</em> shall I bear alone your trouble&rdquo;</span> etc. regarding the rectification of judgment &mdash; for even upon him this matter was exceedingly difficult and weighty, to clarify the judgment. Regarding this he said: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;<em>Aichah</em> shall I bear alone your trouble and your burden and your strife? Provide for yourselves men&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Deuteronomy 1:12&ndash;13)</span>. All of this speaks regarding the rectification of judgment. For Moshe saw how exceedingly difficult and weighty the rectification of judgment is; therefore he said that even he cannot clarify it by himself, and regarding this he said: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;<em>Aichah</em> shall I bear alone&rdquo;</span> etc., as above &mdash; for it is exceedingly difficult to judge alone, as above.
</p>

<p>
And afterward, in the generation of Yeshayah, the judgment was corrupted greatly, and regarding this he cried out: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;<em>Aichah</em> has she become a harlot, the faithful city, she who was filled with justice&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Isaiah 1:21)</span>. For the essential corruption &mdash; that she became a harlot, the aspect of the blemish of the <span class="key-term">bris</span> &mdash; is through the corruption of justice. Through the fact that at first <span class="scripture">&ldquo;she was filled with justice&rdquo;</span> etc., and now <span class="scripture">&ldquo;murderers&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Isaiah 1:21)</span> &mdash; that is, the corruption of judgment. For this depends upon that, as above.
</p>

<p>
And through this the <span class="key-term">Sitra Achara</span> became overpowering until the <span class="key-term">Bais HaMikdash</span> <span class="key-term-def">[Holy Temple]</span> was destroyed. And regarding this Yirmiyah cried out: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;<em>Aichah</em> she sits&rdquo;</span> etc. For the essential destruction came through the blemish of judgment, which is the blemish of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, which is exceedingly difficult to rectify, as above.
</p>

<p>
And the essential rectification is completed only through <span class="key-term">Moshiach</span>, of whom it is said: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;And he shall judge the poor with righteousness etc., he shall judge the destitute of the people&rdquo;</span> etc. <span class="source">(Isaiah 11:4)</span>. And likewise in many verses that speak of the essential rectification of judgment that will come through <span class="key-term">Moshiach</span>. And then it will be fulfilled: <span class="scripture">&ldquo;Tziyon shall be redeemed through justice&rdquo;</span> <span class="source">(Isaiah 1:27)</span>. For the essential <span class="key-term">g&rsquo;ulah</span> <span class="key-term-def">[redemption]</span> will be through the rectification of judgment, through which comes the essential rectification of the <span class="key-term">bris</span>, as above.
</p>

<div class="translator-summary">
<h3>Summary of Hilchos Dayonim 5</h3>
<p>
This short halachah, consisting of five osiyos, develops a single powerful theme from Likutay Moharan II:5: the blemish of judgment (<em>p&rsquo;gam hamishpat</em>) and the blemish of the covenant (<em>p&rsquo;gam habris</em>) are deeply interdependent. When unfit judges pervert justice, or when people rob and defraud one another, the spiritual result is a corruption of the covenant &mdash; and vice versa. This mutual entanglement is what made the rectification of the Generation of the Flood impossible (their decree was sealed on account of robbery, though their sin was primarily sexual), what kept Yisroel in Egypt (Moshe saw the quarreling and understood why the exile persisted), and what eventually led to the destruction of the Bais HaMikdash (Yeshayah cried out: &ldquo;How has she become a harlot, the faithful city that was full of justice!&rdquo;).
</p>
<p>
Three judges are necessary because the task of clarifying judgment requires two opposing movements simultaneously: drawing near (elevating the sparks) and pushing away (rejecting the impure influence of Eisav and Yishmael). One judge inclines right, one inclines left, and the third adjudicates &mdash; corresponding to Yaakov and the three Avos. The ultimate rectification awaits Moshiach, of whom it is said: &ldquo;He shall judge the poor with righteousness&rdquo; &mdash; and then &ldquo;Tziyon shall be redeemed through justice.&rdquo;
</p>
<p class="translator-note">Based on Likutay Moharan II, Siman 5</p>
</div>


